# RABBITS: THE SIGNAL — Core Engineering Rules

## Project Context

* This is RABBITS: THE SIGNAL.
* This is a React Native / Expo / Expo Router / TypeScript mobile game project.
* Target platform for current development is iOS simulator through Expo Go.
* Backend uses Supabase.
* Email infrastructure uses Resend through Supabase SMTP.
* The current repository is `rabbits-ar-game`.
* The active branch is `dev`.
* The local repo path is `/Users/terrancemiles/rabbits-ar-game`.
* Full vision remains intact.
* Full twenty-week season remains canonical.
* Solo operator + advanced AI assistance is the production model.
* Assets are encouraged, not cut.
* Activation is sequenced.
* Los Angeles is final Gate only.
* The player's own city is the center of the game.
* Preserve the full vision.
* Build the full world.
* Activate the proof first.

## Current Product Doctrine

* This is a game, not a generic productivity app.
* The app should feel like a diegetic interface inside the RABBITS world.
* The first priority is proving playable loops.
* Prefer:

  * Player action
  * Persistent state change
  * Game response
* Do not spend time polishing lore if the mechanic is not yet proven.
* Do not remove scope because something seems ambitious.
* Do not argue that the project cannot be solo-operated.
* Do not cut rabbit holes, assets, physical-world extensions, audio, or future Gates.
* Do not make the design Los Angeles-centric except for the final Gate.
* Build small proof layers that preserve the full season structure.

## Source Of Truth

* Source of truth is the code in this repo, not assumptions.
* Read before you write.
* The repository is the source of truth for:

  * Routing
  * Installed packages
  * Existing components
  * Theme tokens
  * Naming patterns
  * Data flow
  * Screen structure
* Do not assume architecture, routing, dependencies, filenames, framework APIs, or package versions.
* Verify against the actual installed versions in this repo.
* If code and simulator disagree, believe the simulator and investigate why.
* If user screenshots disagree with your interpretation, believe the screenshots.

## Before Writing Code

When asked for a non-trivial change, first restate your understanding in 1-3 lines and name the files you expect to touch.

Before coding, explicitly state:

1. What visible user-facing result you are trying to produce.
2. Which file or files you will edit.
3. Which files you will not touch.
4. Whether the change is UI-only, logic-only, dependency-related, database-related, or asset-related.
5. How the change will be verified in the simulator.

If the request is ambiguous, ask before coding.

Never assume a package, API, or version from memory. Verify it exists in this repo by checking `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, and/or the existing imports before importing or calling it.

When using a framework or library API, confirm against the version actually installed. If unsure, say so rather than guessing.

## No Phantom Success

Do not say a feature is done unless it is actually visible and usable in the simulator.

Typecheck passing is not enough.

A change is not complete just because:

* The code was edited.
* The code compiles.
* The file contains the right text.
* The intended button exists somewhere in JSX.
* Metro rebuilt.
* The agent believes it should work.

A change is complete only when:

* The intended UI is visible.
* The intended control is reachable.
* The intended interaction works.
* The simulator shows the expected state.
* The user can perform the intended action.

If you cannot personally verify simulator behavior, say exactly what remains unverified.

## Simulator Is The Truth

The simulator is the truth.

Before declaring success on UI or interaction work, verify the simulator.

For every simulator-facing change, verify:

* The correct screen loads.
* The correct tab is active.
* The changed content is visible.
* Buttons are visible.
* Buttons are tappable.
* Pressing the button causes the intended result.
* Navigation still works.
* The tab bar does not cover critical content.
* No major content is clipped.
* If scrolling is expected, scrolling actually works.

If the simulator still shows old UI:

* Do not assume the wrong file was edited until routing has been verified.
* Check route configuration.
* Check the actual tab file.
* Check whether Metro rebundled.
* Check whether Expo Go is running a stale bundle.
* Restart the simulator project if needed.
* Kill stale Metro processes if needed.
* Reopen the live Development Server entry in Expo Go, not stale Recently Opened entries.

Do not continue feature work while the simulator is stale.

## Expo / Metro Verification

When a change does not appear in Expo Go:

1. Confirm the correct file was edited.
2. Confirm the route uses that file.
3. Confirm Metro shows a fresh `iOS Bundled` line after the change.
4. If not, restart Metro with a cleared cache.
5. If Expo Go still shows stale UI, terminate and reopen Expo Go.
6. Open the project from the live Development Server entry.
7. Do not mistake stale Expo Go cache for missing code.
8. Do not mistake rendered but clipped UI for missing code.

Preferred clean restart command:

```bash
cd /Users/terrancemiles/rabbits-ar-game
npx expo start --clear --ios
```

If port 8081 is stuck:

```bash
lsof -ti tcp:8081 | xargs kill -9
```

Then restart Expo.

## UI Behavior Rules

If the user asks for a button:

* Create a real visible button.
* The button must be visible in the intended screen.
* The button must be reachable without hidden gestures unless explicitly requested.
* The button must have an actual `onPress` behavior.
* The button label must match the requested action.
* The button must not be hidden below clipped content.
* The button must not be hidden behind the tab bar.
* Verify in simulator that the button is visible and tappable.

If the user asks for audio:

* There must be a visible control to play the audio.
* The control must be reachable.
* The control must trigger playback.
* Use the actual bundled asset path.
* Verify the audio dependency exists before importing.
* Verify the audio file exists on disk.
* Verify the simulator can tap the button.
* If sound is not audible, distinguish between:

  * Missing button
  * Broken playback code
  * Missing asset
  * Simulator audio/silent-mode issue
  * Bundle/cache issue
* Do not claim audio works merely because `useAudioPlayer` or similar code exists.

If the user asks for scroll:

* Create a real scrollable container.
* Verify it scrolls in the simulator.
* Verify content below the fold is reachable.
* Verify the last item clears the tab bar.
* Do not say “scroll works” unless a user can actually drag and reveal content.
* If scrolling does nothing, the layout is not fixed.

If the user asks for something to be immediately visible:

* Do not place it below the fold.
* Do not require scrolling.
* Do not bury the primary action inside a clipped card.
* Put the primary action in the first visible area.

If the user says “put the button right there”:

* Place the button in the visible card area near the related text.
* Shorten supporting copy if necessary.
* Do not solve by adding scrolling unless the user explicitly accepts scrolling.

## Layout And Clipping Rules

Never assume content is visible because it exists in JSX.

For every card or panel:

* Check whether the parent container has fixed height.
* Check whether the parent container uses `overflow: hidden`.
* Check whether nested views are clipped.
* Check whether the tab bar overlaps content.
* Check whether a nested `ScrollView` is trapped inside a fixed-height parent.
* Check whether the screen itself can scroll.

If content is clipped:

* Fix the parent layout.
* Do not keep adding content inside the clipped area.
* Do not call the feature complete.
* Do not require the user to guess where the hidden control is.

Primary interactions must be visible, not merely rendered.

## Code & Structure

* Match the patterns of the surrounding files.
* Consistency beats personal preference.
* Keep functions small and single-purpose.
* Prefer early returns over deep nesting.
* Name things for what they do, not how.
* No abbreviations that aren't already in the codebase.
* Put new code where similar code already lives.
* Do not invent new folders without asking.
* Separate concerns:

  * Game logic
  * Rendering
  * Input
  * State
  * Persistence
  * Audio
  * Activation
* No magic numbers. Pull tunable values such as sizes, timings, spacing, and thresholds into named constants or existing theme tokens.
* Write self-documenting code.
* Add comments only to explain why, never what.
* Use existing theme tokens, spacing, colors, radii, typography, and component patterns when available.
* Do not add one-off visual styles unless no suitable pattern exists.

## Editing Behavior

* Produce complete code.
* No stubs.
* No placeholder logic.
* No `// rest of logic here`.
* No TODO placeholders unless explicitly asked for a sketch.
* For files over roughly 400 lines, prefer targeted edits over full rewrites.
* Do not reformat code you were not asked to change.
* Do not “clean up” unrelated code.
* Keep diffs minimal.
* If a change touches other files such as types, callers, tests, config, package manifests, or assets, list what else needs updating.
* Do not silently move features to a different screen.
* Do not silently change the UX requirement to something easier.
* Do not silently substitute “scroll to find it” when the user asked for an immediately visible button.
* Do not silently substitute text-only behavior when the user asked for audio.
* Do not silently substitute a visual placeholder when the user asked for a working interaction.

## Dependency Rules

Before installing a package:

1. Verify it does not already exist.
2. Explain why it is needed.
3. Name the package.
4. Name expected files that will change.
5. Use Expo-compatible installation when appropriate.
6. Do not install alternatives without approval.
7. Do not run broad package upgrades.
8. Do not run `npm audit fix` or `npm audit fix --force` unless explicitly instructed.

For Expo native modules, prefer:

```bash
npx expo install <package-name>
```

Do not use deprecated packages if the current Expo SDK has a preferred replacement.

Do not import from memory. Verify the package API against the installed package or official docs when needed.

## Audio Rules

For bundled audio:

* Asset must exist in the repo.
* Prefer `assets/audio/...` for local audio files.
* Use a stable `require(...)` path from the file that plays it.
* Verify the path from the actual source file location.
* Do not build recording, upload, streaming, or storage unless explicitly requested.
* Do not add Supabase audio storage unless explicitly requested.
* Audio playback must have:

  * Visible play button
  * Clear label
  * Working `onPress`
  * Replay behavior if tapped again
  * No crash when navigating away
* If audio plays but cannot be heard, investigate simulator/device audio mode separately.
* Do not claim audio is working until the user can tap a visible control.

## Routing Rules

Before editing a screen:

* Verify the route file.
* Verify the tab layout.
* Verify whether the visible tab maps to `home.tsx`, `index.tsx`, or another file.
* Do not guess based on filename.
* If a screen is not updating, verify routing before moving code.

For this project:

* Authenticated root redirects to `/home`.
* Home tab is expected to be `src/app/(tabs)/home.tsx`.
* The Index tab is not the Home tab.
* Do not move Home features into the Index tab unless explicitly requested.

## State And Activation Rules

Prefer activation-gated systems.

Use the simplest state source that proves the mechanic:

* Static local state for first proof
* Existing persisted data for activation when available
* Supabase only when persistence is required
* New tables only when the feature needs durable multi-session state

Do not add backend infrastructure for a first visual proof unless required.

Do not create database tables unless the user explicitly approves the schema or asks for persistence.

Do not create fake game state that conflicts with existing profile, report, signal, or index state.

## Game Loop Rules

Every feature should strengthen one of these loops:

1. Player receives signal.
2. Player acts.
3. App records action.
4. App responds.
5. Player investigates further.

Prefer building:

* Dispatches
* Reports
* Signals
* Index entries
* Case file changes
* Field observations
* Audio transmissions
* Activation gates
* Real-world clue hooks

Avoid building:

* Decorative screens with no interaction
* Lore walls with no state
* Menus that do not advance the loop
* Hidden interactions players cannot discover

## RABBITS Canon Rules

* 004773 is one major rabbit hole, not the whole game.
* Solomon is reserved for `RBT-016-000001`.
* Terry is `RBT-016-000002`.
* The experience is asset-rich and activation-gated.
* Full season remains target.
* LA is the final Gate only.
* The player's own city is the center.
* Preserve ambiguity.
* Prefer administrative, accidental, corrupted, procedural, or infrastructural weirdness over obvious fantasy exposition.
